# Every rule the game has, which is three.
#
# **Every rule says what fires it and none of them may leave it out.** `by:command` is a rule a
# player types; `by:turn` is one that fires on its own. A rule that says neither is a rule somebody
# has not finished writing, and the engine refuses it rather than guessing - in both directions,
# so a player cannot type `{grow where:1}` any more than a turn can move the scout.

{rule name:move by:command}

# **The order of `needs` rows is the order they are checked**, so it chooses which refusal a
# reader sees. Destination-is-a-place before destination-is-adjacent, because *there is no
# territory 9* is a better answer than *1 is not next to 9* when both are true.
{needs rule:move relation:at thing:$it place:$from}
{needs rule:move relation:territory id:$to}
{needs rule:move relation:adjacent from:$from to:$to}

# **`$from` needs no `territory` row of its own.** `{at thing:$it place:$from}` above already
# says something is there, and a place holding a thing is a place. A second clause would be a
# row that can never be the one to fail.

# **Burning one fuel, stated as two rows because the engine cannot subtract.** `$had` and `$left`
# are bound by the command and checked against the world: `fuel` says the amount is really what was
# claimed, and `less` says `$left` really is one less than it. Neither can be got at any other way -
# `$name` is substitution and not search, so the engine cannot read the scout's fuel for itself.
#
# **After the adjacency clauses on purpose.** The order of `needs` rows chooses which refusal a
# reader sees, and *you are not next to that* is a better answer than *you cannot afford it* when
# both are true.
{needs rule:move relation:fuel thing:$it amount:$had}
{needs rule:move relation:less of:$had is:$left}

{drops rule:move relation:at thing:$it place:$from}
{adds rule:move relation:at thing:$it place:$to}

{drops rule:move relation:fuel thing:$it amount:$had}
{adds rule:move relation:fuel thing:$it amount:$left}

# The second rule, and the one that tested whether the engine grows when the game does.

{rule name:found by:command}

# **The founder is here, the place is a place, and the place is free.** The third of these is a
# negative - *there is not already a settlement here* - and `needs` can only say *this row is
# true*. So the world states `{vacant place:N}` and this reads it, which is the absence written
# as a fact. Stated without it, the second founding at a place succeeded silently and added a
# second settlement; that is the run this row exists because of.
{needs rule:found relation:at thing:$it place:$where}
{needs rule:found relation:territory id:$where}
{needs rule:found relation:vacant place:$where}

# **Dropping `vacant` is what makes it true only once.** A rule that needs a row and does not drop
# it is a rule that can fire for ever.
{drops rule:found relation:vacant place:$where}
{adds rule:found relation:settlement place:$where owner:$it}

# The third rule, and the first that no player fires.
#
# **Nothing binds `$where` here.** A rule fired by a command takes its holes from the command, and
# this one has no command - so the engine has to find every settlement itself and fire once for
# each. That is the search the first three concepts did without, and it is where `src/` finally
# grew.

{rule name:grow by:turn}

# **Two clauses, and the second is what makes this a join.** `$where` and `$who` are bound by the
# first clause and *filter* the second: a settlement grows while its owner is still standing in it,
# and one whose owner has walked away does not. With one clause the engine could bind each hole
# independently and nothing would notice - `README.md` says which run showed that.
{needs rule:grow relation:settlement place:$where owner:$who}
{needs rule:grow relation:at thing:$who place:$where}

{adds rule:grow relation:food place:$where}
